@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy";
}

<h1 class="h3">Privacy</h1>
<p class="text-muted">
    What The War Room keeps about the people who use it, who can see it, and what stays in your own browser.
</p>

<div class="row">
    <div class="col-lg-9">
        <h2 class="h5 mt-3">Your team's workspace is private</h2>
        <p>
            Every team works in its own workspace. Nothing a team enters can be seen by another team:
            not its roster, its depth charts, its playbook, its film or its schedule.
            The head coach creates the accounts of the team's coaches and players and decides what each coach may do.
        </p>

        <h2 class="h5 mt-4">What is kept</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Your account:</strong> your name, your email address and your role. Your password is stored as a hash, never as the password itself.</li>
            <li><strong>Roster details for players:</strong> jersey number, grade, height, weight and status, as entered by the coaches, and the position groups you belong to.</li>
            <li><strong>Depth charts, the schedule and attendance:</strong> where each player stands each week, the team's events, and the attendance coaches record when the head coach has switched tracking on.</li>
            <li><strong>The playbook:</strong> formations, plays, and every change request with who made it and who decided on it.</li>
            <li><strong>Film records:</strong> the games on film, the plays cut from them and how they are tagged, the cutups coaches build, and who each cutup is shared with.</li>
            <li><strong>When you were signed in:</strong> the start of each sign-in and the time of your last activity in it.</li>
            <li><strong>Study time for players:</strong> how long you actively viewed playbook pages and shared film.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Study time</h2>
        <p>
            Players see a study timer on playbook pages. It counts only while the page is in front of you and you are using it,
            and it shows whether it is running or paused. Time signed in, film time and playbook time are kept as three separate numbers.
            They show activity. They are not a grade, there are no deadlines, and they are not proof of learning.
        </p>
        <p>
            You can see your own numbers at any time under "My study time".
            Your head coach sees every player's numbers; a coordinator or position coach sees those of the players in their groups.
            Other players do not see yours.
        </p>

        <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Who can see what</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Everyone on a team can see the roster names, the depth chart and the schedule.</li>
            <li>Players see the approved playbooks of the units they play in, the film that was shared with them, and their own attendance and study time.</li>
            <li>Coaches see attendance and film, and the study time of the players in their groups.</li>
            <li>Accounts, roles and permissions are managed by the head coach only.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="h5 mt-4">What stays in your browser</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>A sign-in cookie, which ends by itself after 30 minutes without activity or when you sign out.</li>
            <li>A cookie that protects the site's forms against requests made from other sites.</li>
            <li>In the play designer, a copy of a drawing you have not saved yet, so it is not lost if your sign-in runs out. It stays on that device and is removed when the drawing is saved.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The site uses no advertising or tracking services, and it sends no email.</p>

        <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Leaving a team</h2>
        <p>
            Accounts are deactivated by the head coach rather than deleted, so that attendance, playbook history and other
            records of the team stay complete. A deactivated account can no longer sign in.
            Ask your head coach if something that is kept about you is wrong.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
